-- title:
--   Finiteness of K(j)bigl(j(q^N)bigr) over K(j)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $N$ be a natural number with $N \neq 0$, and let `data` be a `ModularPolynomialData N`, that is: a polynomial $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ which is monic in $Y$, whose degree in $Y$ equals $\psi(N) = \sum_{d \mid N,\ d \text{ squarefree}} N/d$, and which satisfies $\Phi(j, j_N) = 0$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb{Q})$, where the coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}[X]$ are evaluated at the rational $q$-expansion $j$ via `evalAtJ` and the outer variable at `jqN N`. Inside the Laurent series field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(K)$ consider the element $\mathrm{jqModC}\,K = q^{-1} \cdot (E_4^3 \cdot \eta\text{-unit-inverse})$, the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant with coefficients pushed from $\mathbb{Z}$ to $K$, and $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,K\,N$, its image under the ring homomorphism `qExpand` of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(K)$ which multiplies all exponents by $N$ (substitution $q \mapsto q^N$). The assertion is that the intermediate field obtained by adjoining $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,K\,N$ to $E = K(\mathrm{jqModC}\,K)$ is finite-dimensional over $E$. Only finiteness is asserted; no numerical bound on the degree appears in the conclusion.
--
--   This is the statement that $j(q^N)$ is algebraic over the field generated by $j$ over $K$, the finiteness underlying the description of the function field of $X_0(N)$ as a finite extension of $K(j)$. It is used by the places-and-degrees computations for that function field, for instance in the cuspidal divisor class and local-chart statements that compute sums of $e \cdot f$ over $K(j)$.
